#X text 150 690 turn 19 (2026-09-18 \, per-voice sample parameters / reverse playback): no code change needed in this abstraction for reverse playback -- a negative rate already works correctly through the existing rate/loopEnd/loopStart list. Verified via 3 probes before touching any real file: (1) [phasor~ -10] produces a clean descending ramp \, not silence or garbage \; (2) a direct tabwrite~ sample-level capture after an explicit phase-0 reset with negative freq showed sample[0]=0 \, sample[1]=0.999792 -- phase wraps immediately to just-under-1 and descends \, it does not go negative/unwrapped \; (3) [tabread4] at negative/near-zero indices clamps (constant output) rather than crashing or reading garbage \, confirming the mirrored seam artifact at loopStart in reverse mode is the same class of known limitation as the existing loopEnd seam above \, not a new crash risk. End-to-end verified against real loaded audio through this actual abstraction (not just phasor~ in isolation): tests/test_play_loop_reverse.pd + tests/run_play_loop_reverse_test.sh \, sending "-1 4410 0" instead of "1 4410 0". Correlation 1.000000 against the reversed ramp \, mean error 0.001%. One caveat baked into that test rather than hidden: the existing bang/reset-to-phase-0 retrigger mechanism (unchanged) means the very first captured sample after a reverse retrigger reads the loop-START value for exactly one sample before the ramp jumps to loop-end and starts descending -- a single-sample artifact \, same category as the existing tabread4~ seam limitation \, not masked out of the test but explicitly asserted on. Confirmed unclamped all the way up the call chain too: sample_voice~.pd's unpack/pack chain and file_poly.pd's play/playv dispatch apply max/min clamping only to the slot number (0..7) \, never to rate -- a negative rate was already reaching here with zero plumbing changes needed.;
#X obj 10 730 float 1;
#X obj 150 730 float 1;
#X obj 10 770 t b b b;
#X obj 10 810 expr abs($i1);
#X obj 150 810 /;
#X obj 150 850 /;
#X obj 150 890 * 1000;
#X obj 10 850 delay 0;
#X obj 250 450 outlet;
#X text 250 490 turn 22 (2026-09-18 \, SHOT mode item): "done" bang out this NEW outlet1 (outlet order is by x-coordinate per gotcha #10 -- this object sits at x=250 \, right of outlet~'s x=10 \, so it's outlet1 not outlet0) \, fired once per retrigger \, timed to land at the moment phasor~ would complete one full cycle. No natural "stop instead of wrap" mechanism exists in phasor~/tabread4~ (probed \, confirmed absent) \, so this is a SCHEDULED TIMER \, not a detector: duration_ms = range / abs(rate) / sampleRate * 1000 \, reusing the exact period_samples = range/rate relationship already validated during turn 19's reverse-playback work (sr cancels out of that ratio by construction). Armed on every retrigger bang (the SAME event that resets phase to 0) via a [t b b b] that (1) loads abs(rate) into the first division's cold inlet \, (2) triggers range/absRate -> /sampleRate -> *1000 into [delay]'s time inlet \, (3) starts the delay -- three strictly-ordered steps \, right-to-left per gotcha #4. [delay]'s own re-trigger-cancels-pending-fire behavior (standard \, not this abstraction's own logic) means a mid-flight retrigger correctly restarts the timer rather than firing twice. This abstraction still doesn't know about "mode" (LOOP vs SHOT) at all -- it just offers the done-bang unconditionally \; sample_voice~.pd decides whether to act on it.;
